tests:
  unit: { count: 36, covers: "The readiness rule mr confirm applies before a human is asked to sign, over the three states Gated now names. A gated tip that changes no code owes its test/v2 and doc/v1 and no review - the case that refused this WU. The same tip still owes those two notes, so the fix narrows which evidence a codeless tip owes and never whether it owes any. A code-bound note on a codeless tip never reaches the verdict, because tip_unready filters it exactly where check_review would drop it - so a stale or failing cr/v5 nobody was entitled to write cannot block a signature. A legacy whole-bound cr/v2 or cr/v3 IS judged on that same arm, verdict and binding both, because the gate judges it whatever the diff touched: the review of this very commit found the first cut of the fix skipping it and signing a tip verify-dod refuses. The two pre-existing cases - a gated tip with nothing on it, and an unresolvable target - re-pointed at the enum, the second asserting that a range nobody can resolve still demands its review. No test here touches a repo: the rule is pure." }
  integration: { count: 64, covers: "The contract end to end over a real temporary git tree, because the bug was in how mr confirm derives its range and not in the predicate it feeds - a pure test would have passed against the broken code. A branch carrying one closed design run (data-artifact=design-run, data-status=archived) and no code signs with test/v2 and doc/v1 alone: exit 0, and the mr/v1 note it was refusing is on the tip. The other direction is asserted too, so a predicate that always answered 'no review' cannot pass the suite: the same branch with a code path added is refused, naming the missing review. Third, from this commit's own review: a legacy cr/v3 FAIL note on a codeless design tip still blocks the signature, because the gate still judges a whole-bound schema there. Two of the three were run against the code they refuse - the first reproduces the exact refusal this WU hit, the third reproduces the signature the first cut of the fix would wrongly have written." }
  e2e: { waived: "Two reasons, and neither is the design half's. The epic's e2e lands at W4 against the built screen, and a design doc renders no surface for a driver to touch - the ground W1 waived on. The mr fix's own end-to-end seam is the merge that follows this note, which cannot be asserted from inside the range it gates; xtask/tests/e2e_design_flow.rs already drives a closed run through 9-design, and the result's follow-up 3 records that nothing drives one through mr confirm - deliberately left as a roadmap row rather than smuggled in here." }
